<h3>Raids and Preparation<o:p></o:p></h3>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;mso-margin-top-alt:auto;mso-margin-bottom-alt:
auto;margin-left:.5in'>Bloodhorde plans to have raids of various dungeons and
zones from time to time. The main rule for raids is <u>be prepared</u>. All
raids will be announced at least 5 days in advance. If you plan on being at the
raid, be there on time and come prepared. For preparation, we suggest visiting
(if possible) the place prior to the raid to know its location and layout. <u>We
strongly suggest that you print out a map of the raid zone so you do not get
lost</u>. If we say &quot;everybody meet at the jail room on the 3rd
floor&quot;, your answer should be &quot;ok&quot;, not &quot;how do I get
there?&quot;. <o:p></o:p></p>

<p style='margin-right:.5in;margin-left:.5in'>Nothing wastes more time and
risks more lives than an individual that does not know the zone. Getting lost
leads to one thing, death, and death can quickly turn a fun raid into a
miserable experience. If it is determined that an individual has come to the
raid ill-prepared, they will be asked to leave. <o:p></o:p></p>

<p style='margin-right:.5in;margin-left:.5in'>Also note that the gain of
experience is the absolutely last concern on a raid. Raids often have 2 or more
groups of people. Therefore, it is unlikely that any one group is going to get
XP for every kill since all groups will most likely be fighting the same MoB.
Also, you may be grouped with somebody well beyond your level, which would
cause you to get very little XP. Raids are not for XP. Raids are to go in and
kill many things, obtain treasure, and have fun. Whining about not getting
experience during a raid is considered very bad form. If you want experience,
don't come to a raid. However, if you want a chance at some good items and have
fun, thats what a raid is about<o:p></o:p></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><![if !supportEmptyParas]>&nbsp;<![endif]><o:p></o:p></p>

